<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    THE CHARTER: Core is the substrate — what a space is, how it is read, the facets a space may
    offer, and the reference grid. Nothing else.

    Every type here is a contract, a type a contract speaks, a facet a space may add, or the one
    in-memory space that stands in for a backend:

      ISpace, the strategy and scan interfaces,
      ILineLandmark                                                  the contracts themselves
      Plane                                                          the 2-D locator: the region a
                                                                     strategy is shown, spoken by
                                                                     every scan and landmark
      Point                                                          the 0-D locator Plane mints
      CanonicalReads                                                 ISpace's questions asked of a Point
      Scans                                                          the fold that defines a
                                                                     strategy's whole-region answer
      Size, Offset, Size, Orientation, OffsetStep                    the strategy signatures
      OutOfBoundsException                                           the throw ISpace mandates
      ScanContractException                                          the throw Scans mandates of a
                                                                     scan that broke its contract
      CellProblem, TextReads                                         what a read hands back when it
                                                                     refuses, and the parsing default
                                                                     a text-backed space may use
      IValueSpace, ValueReads, IFormulaSpace, FormulaReads           the facets a space may offer,
                                                                     each with its point half
      GridSpace                                                      the reference in-memory space

    Hashes, Renderings and Spans are internal and exempt: the charter is about what this project's
    contracts SPEAK, and nothing outside this package can name them. Hashes exists because the value
    types here — and the cell struct in Unrect.Spreadsheets, which is granted the same reach — mix a
    hash the same way, and System.HashCode is netstandard2.1 and up. Renderings, because the
    in-memory grid and the spreadsheet cell must write a double as the same digits, and neither
    target's own rendering agrees with the other's. Spans, because it is the arithmetic every scan
    and machine would otherwise write for itself.

    A type that merely USES those contracts belongs above them, in Unrect.Projections: a node, a
    machine, a view, a diagnostic.

    The test before adding a type: is it part of what a space IS, how one is read, or a facet one
    may offer? If not, it goes in Unrect.Projections — which ships in the same package, so nothing
    about the move reaches a consumer but the namespace.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;netstandard2.1</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The internal helpers — the span arithmetic every scan and machine shares, the one double
         rendering, the one sentence a scan that breaks its contract is refused with — are the parts
         of Core the packages above it are written with and a consumer never touches. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Unrect" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Unrect.Engine" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Unrect.Strategies" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Unrect.Spreadsheets" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Unrect.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
